\section{How to use it}{
\strong{1. Point at a library.} Every function takes \verb{root =}. Omit it and the root is
resolved through a documented precedence chain whose \emph{winning stage} is reported,
so a machine that resolves the wrong root can be diagnosed instead of guessed at:

\preformatted{cfg <- datalib_config()
cfg$datalib       # the root
cfg$source_stage  # argument | env | option | config_generic | config_package | unset}

\strong{2. Find the vintage you want.} Version defaults are \emph{numeric-latest}, never
alphabetical, and omitting \code{year} or \code{survey} means "the newest":

\preformatted{datalib_resolve("ZWE", 2019, "MICS", collection = "HLT")   # latest HLT adaptation
datalib_resolve("ZWE", 2019, "MICS", kind = "master")      # latest master
datalib_resolve("KEN")                                     # latest survey, latest vintage}

\strong{3. Load it.} Merge keys come from the collection registry
(\code{config/collections.yml}), never from guessing. Person-level modules chain 1:1,
then household-level modules attach m:1; a module whose keys do not uniquely
identify its rows stops the merge rather than silently multiplying rows:

\preformatted{datalib_load("ZWE", 2019, "MICS", collection = "HLT",
             module = c("household", "children"))}
}

\section{Two rules worth knowing before you rely on the results}{
\strong{Enumerators return empty; resolvers raise.} Asking a country for a survey it
does not have is a legitimate question with the answer "none", so
\code{\link[=datalib_surveys]{datalib_surveys()}} gives you zero rows. Asking \code{\link[=datalib_resolve]{datalib_resolve()}} for a vintage
that does not exist is a mistake, so it errors. "No results" and "you asked about
something that does not exist" are different problems and must not look the same.

\strong{Errors are typed.} Every failure carries a condition class -- match on the
class, not on the English message:

\preformatted{tryCatch(datalib_resolve("XXX", 2019, "MICS"),
         datalib_error_not_found = function(e) NULL)}

Classes: \code{datalib_error_input}, \code{datalib_error_not_found},
\code{datalib_error_root_unset}, \code{datalib_error_config_missing},
\code{datalib_error_user_missing}, all inheriting \code{datalib_error}.
}

\section{Installing and upgrading}{
Not on CRAN; the LAN share is the supported path. \strong{Note the \verb{utils::} prefix} --
plain \code{install.packages()} installs correctly but then errors in RStudio, whose
hook mistakes the tarball path for an installed-package directory:

\preformatted{utils::install.packages("Z:/_pkg/datalib/R/datalib_0.9.30.tar.gz",
                        repos = NULL, type = "source")}

\code{repos = NULL} resolves no dependencies, so on a fresh machine install
\code{yaml}, \code{haven} and \code{fs} first. \code{\link[=datalib_update]{datalib_update()}} reports whether something newer
exists but deliberately does \strong{not} install: \code{install.packages()} over a loaded
namespace is unsafe on Windows, where the running session locks the package's own
files. Install, then restart the session -- the R equivalent of Stata's
\code{discard}. See the R README for the full account.
}

